### The Syntax of Implementation

A class adopts an interface using the `implements` keyword:
```java
public class Car implements Drivable {
    @Override
    public void drive() { ... }
}
```

### The Strict Visibility Rule
Because all interface methods are implicitly `public`, any class implementing them **MUST** declare the overriding methods as `public`:
```java
interface Greeter {
    void greet(); // implicitly public abstract
}

class FriendlyPerson implements Greeter {
    // COMPILATION ERROR! Cannot reduce visibility from public to package-private
    void greet() { System.out.println("Hi"); }

    // CORRECT:
    @Override
    public void greet() { System.out.println("Hi"); }
}
```

### Combining `extends` and `implements`
A class can extend **one** superclass and implement **one or more** interfaces simultaneously. In the class declaration, `extends` **must come before** `implements`:
```java
// CORRECT ORDER: extends SuperClass implements Interface1, Interface2
public class Dog extends Canine implements Pet, Trainable {
    ...
}

// COMPILATION ERROR: implements before extends is illegal syntax!
public class Dog implements Pet extends Canine { } // ERROR
```

